use ../cli
from ../analysis import Analysis
from ../analysis/Output import Output
from ../analysis/measures/Readiness import Readiness
from ./Check import Check

export cli-command Analyze:
    commandName: analyze

    positionalArguments:
        paths:
            Files, directories or quoted globs, as for @{Check}. With none, the
            whole project is analyzed.

    namedArguments:
        view: What to show, as listed in @{Output}. Defaults to overview.
        scope: Narrow the view to an entity, part, statement, fact or open item.
        format: text, markdown, json or html. Defaults to text.
        out: Write the view to this file instead of standard output.
        strict: Exit non-zero if anything in scope is not implementation-ready.
        no-examples: Do not compile code examples, which makes the run faster.

    description:
        Analyzes what a Piton specification actually says: the facts it
        states and implies, what it leaves undefined or ambiguous, where it
        contradicts itself, how much an implementer would still have to
        invent, and the explicit instructions it amounts to. What analysis is
        and how it works is described in @{Analysis}.

        ```sh
        piton analyze                                   the whole project
        piton analyze spec/shape                        one directory
        piton analyze --scope Button                    one entity
        piton analyze --view gaps                       only what is undefined
        piton analyze --view facts --format json        the model, for tools
        piton analyze --view instructions --scope Button
        piton analyze --view trace --scope Button.disabled/s3
        piton analyze --strict                          fail CI below the line
        ```

    wholeProjectOrFiles:
        With no paths, analyze finds piton.config.pi the way @{Check} does
        and analyzes every file under the project root, including files the
        entry never reaches, so that those can be reported. With paths, only
        the matching files are analyzed, but the files they import are still
        read, so that names resolve. Whether a file is reached from the entry
        is only known in a whole-project analysis.

    exitStatus:
        Analyze exits with 0 when it produces its output, whatever the output
        says. With strict, it exits with 1 when any entity in scope is not
        implementation-ready, as described in @{Readiness}. It exits with 2
        when it cannot produce output: nothing matched, there is no project to
        analyze, or the out file cannot be written.
